Whether this document states any result of its own is a question about the whole document, never
about one line of it. A document that restates or indexes material stated authoritatively
elsewhere states no result of its own: a notation table, a glossary, a reference list, a section
preamble. Its lines read as assertions, and each one recalls a result some other document states
first — a table of symbols is such a document however many of its rows would make sentences.

Where the document states no result of its own, your answer carries no claim block and no paragraph
claim. It carries instead exactly one block of this kind, holding one sentence on one line, beside
any not-a-claim verdicts it owes:

    @!no-claim-open!@
    This section fixes the notation the rest of the volume uses and states no result of its own.
    @!no-claim-close!@

That sentence is permanent: it is written into this document's frontmatter and is what a later
reader is told about why this document carries no result. Say what the document is and what that
makes of its lines. Write it yourself, and do not hand back the sentence already in this
document's frontmatter: that one says only that the document marks no claim block, and nothing
about what its prose states.

Where the document states even one result, return its claims and no block of this kind. An answer
carrying claims together with this block is not an answer, and neither is one carrying neither.